(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"avatar-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Road Through the Hills","subtitle":"Jasmine After Rain · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"The road through the hills was beautiful in the way difficult things sometimes were.\n\nPine trees covered the slopes. Water rushed through narrow valleys. Mist collected in the mornings and turned the world silver. The cart wheels complained over stone, and Iroh insisted the sound was a form of music.\n\n“Then the cart is a very unhappy musician,” I said.\n\nThe cart proved my point less than an hour later. Its left wheel hit a rut, and a sharp wooden knock sounded beneath us. Iroh asked the driver to stop. He was out of the seat before I could untangle myself from the travel blanket.\n\n“Do you intend to interrogate the wheel?” I asked.\n\n“If necessary. It has made a very serious accusation.”\n\nThe driver, a narrow-faced woman named Yulan, crouched beside the axle and took one look at the split pin. “Nobody rides another mile with that,” she said. “The load will take the wheel off on the next descent.”\n\nIroh knelt beside her. He had already found a length of spare timber in the cart, and I recognized the look he wore when he was about to solve a problem with confidence and an alarming amount of ingenuity.\n\nYulan put her hand over the wood. “That brace works for a stall roof. This axle needs an iron pin. I packed one.”\n\nFor a heartbeat I thought he might argue. He looked at the slope, then set the timber aside.\n\n“Then I owe you an apology for nearly making your repair harder.”\n\n“You can make it easier by holding the lantern,” she told him.\n\nHe did. No stories about old generals or valuable lessons while she worked; just a man keeping the light steady as Yulan fitted a pin with a small hammer. I held the spare wheel chock, cold grit soaking through my sleeve.\n\nWhen we climbed back onto the seat, Iroh tucked the unusable timber under his boots.\n\n“Keeping a souvenir?” I asked.\n\n“A reminder that I may be an excellent tactician and a terrible wheelwright.”\n\n“You're also the world's least subtle lantern holder.”\n\n“I thought my lantern work quite dignified.”\n\nYulan called over her shoulder, “It was fine. Next time, hold it lower.”\n\nI leaned against him as the cart rolled on. His laugh warmed the space between our shoulders, and when he took my hand beneath the blanket, the contact felt less like being looked after and more like being invited along.\n\n“It has experienced hardship.”\n\n“It needs a lesson in maintenance.”\n\n“Perhaps you should teach it.”\n\n“Perhaps you should stop arguing with objects.”\n\n“The cart began the argument.”\n\nI had been traveling for four days. The work was going well in small, exhausting pieces. Two governors had agreed to share water routes. A former commander had signed a proposal to return seized land. Another had refused to meet and sent a messenger who threw a stone at the tea chest.\n\n“Did you expect it to be easy?” I asked while repairing the broken latch.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did you expect to fix everything?”\n\nIroh knelt beside me. “I hoped to help with something.”\n\n“That is different.”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\nThe latch refused to close. I tightened the screw.\n\n“Hold it,” I said.\n\nIroh held it.\n\n“No, not like that.”\n\nHe adjusted his hand.\n\n“Still wrong.”\n\n“I am doing my best.”\n\n“Your best is currently holding a piece of metal upside down.”\n\n“It is a complicated piece of metal.”\n\nI laughed. The sound echoed against the hills.\n\nIroh smiled at me, and the frustration left the morning.\n\nThat afternoon, the rain returned. The path became slick. The cart tilted when a wheel slipped into mud.\n\nIroh jumped down to push. I climbed beside him.\n\n“Stay in the cart,” he said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“The ground is dangerous.”\n\n“So is carrying everything alone.”\n\nHe looked at me, then nodded.\n\nTogether, I pushed until the wheel came free. Mud covered my clothes. Iroh's hair had fallen from its tie. He looked nothing like a general and everything like a man trying to move a stubborn cart through the weather.\n\n“You are laughing,” he said.\n\n“You have mud on your nose.”\n\n“You also have mud on your nose.”\n\n“That makes it fair.”\n\nThe nearest shelter was an abandoned way station. The roof held. The walls did not leak too badly. I built a fire while Iroh changed into dry clothes from the trunk.\n\nWhen he returned, he carried two cups of tea.\n\n“You brought tea into a storm,” I said.\n\n“Some priorities must survive.”\n\nI accepted the cup. Steam warmed my face.\n\nThe rain hammered the roof. The way station smelled of wet wool and smoke.\n\n“Do you regret bringing me?” I asked.\n\nIroh sat beside me on the blanket. “No.”\n\n“You answer quickly.”\n\n“Some questions deserve a quick answer.”\n\n“What if I am slowing you down?”\n\n“Then we will travel more slowly.”\n\n“What if I am a liability?”\n\n“Then we will plan more carefully.”\n\n“What if I am only here because you did not want to leave me behind?”\n\nIroh turned toward me. “I asked you to come because your judgment is valuable. I asked because I enjoy your company. I asked because I wanted to see what you would choose when the road became inconvenient.”\n\n“And what did I choose?”\n\n“You climbed into the mud.”\n\n“That is not very romantic.”\n\n“It was deeply romantic to me.”\n\nI leaned against his shoulder. He wrapped his cloak around both of us.\n\n“You do not have to earn your place beside me by being useful,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\n“I’m working on it.”\n\n“Then work slowly.”\n\nThe storm lasted through the night. I and Iroh took turns feeding the fire. At some point, I fell asleep with my head in his lap.\n\nWhen I woke, his hand was resting in my hair.\n\n“You were awake?” I asked.\n\n“For part of the night.”\n\n“Why didn’t you move?”\n\n“You looked comfortable.”\n\n“You could have woken me.”\n\n“I considered it.”\n\n“And?”\n\n“I decided the world could wait.”\n\nThe rain had stopped. Morning light entered through the broken shutters.\n\nI sat up. Iroh's hand fell away.\n\n“Still comfortable?” he asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Still wish to continue?”\n\nI touched his face. “Yes.”\n\nHe kissed me in the quiet way station. His mouth was warm. His hands held me close beneath the cloak.\n\nWhen the kiss deepened, he paused to ask. I answered. He smiled against my mouth.\n\nThe rest of the morning remained private, hidden from the road and the rain.\n\nAfterward, I repaired the latch.\n\nIt closed on the first attempt.\n\nIroh stared at it. “You have performed a miracle.”\n\n“I tightened a screw.”\n\n“Miracles are often misunderstood.”\n\nI packed the tea chests. He hitched the cart.\n\nThe road through the hills continued north.\n\nthe two of us followed it together, muddy, tired, and laughing.\n\nAfter the storm, the road became quiet for several miles. The trees dripped. Water ran in bright threads along the stones. Iroh drove the cart while I sat beside him with the repaired latch in my lap.\n\n“You are holding it like a trophy,” he said.\n\n“It defeated us.”\n\n“It was a latch.”\n\n“A stubborn latch.”\n\n“You are very loyal to objects once they annoy you.”\n\n“You are very loyal to people once they hurt you.”\n\nIroh glanced at me. “That was a sharper observation.”\n\n“I am practicing.”\n\n“I am proud.”\n\nI looked at the road. “Do you think loyalty is always good?”\n\n“No. Loyalty without judgment can become another form of fear.”\n\n“What should it be?”\n\n“A choice renewed. Not a chain inherited.”\n\nThe cart rolled over a bridge. The stream beneath it was swollen from the storm.\n\n“Would you still choose me if I stopped being useful?” I asked.\n\nIroh did not answer immediately.\n\n“Yes,” he said.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because you are not useful to me. You are beloved.”\n\nThe words were so direct that I forgot the road for a moment.\n\n“That sounds like a line you have been saving.”\n\n“I have been waiting for the right weather.”\n\n“The weather is muddy.”\n\n“It is honest.”\n\nI leaned my head against his shoulder.\n\nAt the next village, I stopped to replace the damaged tea leaves. The shopkeeper asked why Iroh was traveling with so many chests. He explained the negotiations. The shopkeeper offered directions to a mountain spring and a warning about the bridge ahead.\n\n“The bridge is out,” she said. “Take the forest path.”\n\nIroh thanked her and bought three bundles of tea.\n\n“You did not need more,” I said.\n\n“She gave us useful information.”\n\n“You are bribing every person with leaves.”\n\n“It is not bribery if everyone enjoys the exchange.”\n\nThe forest path was narrow but dry. I walked beside the cart, guiding the wheel around roots. Iroh let me choose the pace.\n\n“You trust me with the road,” I said.\n\n“I trust you to tell me when the road is unsafe.”\n\n“That is different.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“You could trust me more.”\n\nIroh smiled. “I could.”\n\n“Will you?”\n\n“I am.”\n\nThe answer was enough.\n\nAt sunset, the forest opened onto a valley full of wildflowers. I stopped the cart. Iroh climbed down.\n\n“This is beautiful,” I said.\n\n“It is.”\n\n“You knew it was here.”\n\n“I hoped.”\n\nHe took my hand. the two of us walked into the flowers, leaving the cart behind.\n\nIroh kissed me beneath the evening sky. There was no storm, no argument, no urgent decision. Only the quiet pleasure of finding something beautiful after difficulty.\n\nWhen I returned to the cart, he tucked a small flower behind my ear.\n\n“You are showing off,” I said.\n\n“I am making a memory.”\n\nI kept the flower until it dried.\n\nWhen the night grew colder, I returned to the cart and built a small fire. Iroh wrapped the spare blanket around my shoulders. I protested that he was giving me all the warmth.\n\n“I have firebending,” he said.\n\n“That is not a substitute for a blanket.”\n\n“It is a very effective substitute.”\n\n“You are not allowed to set yourself on fire for romance.”\n\n“I had not planned to.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHe sat beside me, close enough that my arms touched.\n\n“You are worried about my safety,” he said.\n\n“You are traveling through a politically unstable region with a cart full of tea.”\n\n“A dangerous combination.”\n\n“You say that like it is funny.”\n\n“It is a little funny.”\n\n“You are impossible.”\n\n“You have said this before.”\n\n“I am consistent.”\n\n“A valuable quality.”\n\nThe fire snapped. Iroh looked at the flower in my notebook.\n\n“You kept it,” he said.\n\n“It is evidence.”\n\n“Of what?”\n\n“That you can find something beautiful after a difficult day.”\n\nHe touched the page. “You are learning to keep the right evidence.”\n\nI leaned into him. The night settled around the camp. Neither of us hurried toward sleep.\n\nThe road could wait until sunrise.\n\nI was not in a hurry.\n\nThe flowers waited in the notebook, the cart waited on the road, and Iroh waited beside me.\n\nTogether, we watched night settle over the pine trees. In the morning I pressed the last flower flat against the notebook page before we climbed back into the cart.","word_count":1859},"avatar-17":{"number":17,"title":"Return to Ba Sing Se","subtitle":"Jasmine After Rain · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"Ba Sing Se greeted me with noise.\n\nThe market had expanded while I was gone. New signs hung over old shops. Children ran between carts. The walls still bore scars, but jasmine had climbed higher.\n\nIroh stood beside me at the city gate, looking toward the tea shop.\n\n“Do you want to go directly there?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you want to stop for food?”\n\n“Also yes.”\n\n“You can have both.”\n\n“This is why I brought you.”\n\nThe cart returned to the Jasmine Dragon by afternoon. The shop sign had been repainted. The windows were clean. A new shelf stood behind the counter.\n\nThe substitute tea master greeted Iroh with a bow and immediately complained about the kettle.\n\nMina came out of the storeroom with a ledger under one arm and a tea-stained apron over the other.\n\n\"The east kettle leaks, Ren has worked three extra afternoons, and we owe the tile maker for the roof,\" she said. \"Which problem would you like first?\"\n\n\"The most urgent,\" Iroh replied, with a former general's attention.\n\n\"Then sign Ren's pay. I already gave him tomorrow off.\"\n\nIroh reached for the ledger. Mina held on to it.\n\n\"Don't announce a generous holiday,\" she said. \"He earned the hours. Put the wages in writing.\"\n\nI pressed my lips together. Iroh caught my expression.\n\n\"You seem delighted by my return to civilian command.\"\n\n\"Somebody has to keep you in line.\"\n\nMina showed him the invoice, separating labor from materials. He checked the amounts and signed instead of trying to pay her with gratitude.\n\nThen she handed me a small box of dry tea tins instead of the heavy crate.\n\n\"I can carry the other one,\" I said.\n\n\"You can,\" Mina agreed. \"You needn't.\"\n\nIroh smiled. \"She knows you already.\"\n\nWhat pleased me was not only that the shop had survived without him. It was that he allowed it to belong to everyone who had kept it alive. Mina went to eat her own lunch when the ledger closed, and nobody called her back.\n\n“It has become impossible,” she said.\n\n“It has always been impossible,” Iroh replied.\n\nShe looked at me. “You are in charge of it now.”\n\n“I was afraid of that.”\n\nThe shop filled with customers who wanted to hear about the journey. Iroh told them only what they needed to know. I served tea, answered questions, and watched his shoulders lower as the familiar rhythm returned.\n\nThat evening, after closing, he stood behind the counter with both hands resting on the wood.\n\n“I missed this,” he said.\n\n“The shop?”\n\n“The ordinary parts.”\n\nI stepped beside him. “I missed your tea.”\n\n“You made tea while I was gone.”\n\n“It was not the same.”\n\n“Practice.”\n\n“You are going to make me do it again.”\n\n“I am.”\n\nHe poured two cups. The jasmine scent filled the room.\n\n“I received your letters,” I said.\n\n“I wrote them.”\n\n“You wrote five.”\n\n“I was busy.”\n\n“The first one was a report about a governor.”\n\n“A fascinating governor.”\n\n“The second was a complaint about rice.”\n\n“Bad rice deserves documentation.”\n\n“The third said you missed me.”\n\nIroh's ears turned pink.\n\n“It did,” he admitted.\n\n“The fourth said you wished I were there.”\n\n“Also true.”\n\n“The fifth said you had learned how to sleep late.”\n\n“A triumph.”\n\nI lifted my cup. “You forgot to say you loved me.”\n\n“I was saving it for your return.”\n\nHe moved around the counter. “I love you.”\n\nThe words were simple enough to fill the shop.\n\nI placed my cup down. “I love you too.”\n\nIroh drew me close, and we shared a gentle kiss in the quiet shop. We paused to check that we were both comfortable, then stayed together as the tea cooled.\n\nWhen he paused, I touched his cheek.\n\n“Still comfortable?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Still certain?”\n\n“Ask me tomorrow.”\n\nHis smile became bright. “You have learned to use my own lessons against me.”\n\n“It was inevitable.”\n\nThe shop door rattled in the wind. Neither of us moved.\n\n“The sign is still turned to closed,” I said.\n\n“It has excellent manners.”\n\nHe kissed me again. The counter pressed lightly against my back. Iroh's hand moved slowly, asking through every pause. I answered by drawing him closer.\n\nWe talked on the balcony while the tea cooled. When we chose to go upstairs together, we left the rest of the evening private.\n\nWe stayed above the shop until late, talking through the journey while rain began against the balcony.\n\nIn the morning, the jasmine plant had flowered.\n\nIroh found me standing by the window.\n\n“It is showing off,” he said.\n\n“It missed you.”\n\n“It has been cared for.”\n\n“So have you.”\n\nHe came behind me and wrapped his arms around my waist.\n\n“Yes,” he said. “I have.”\n\nThe shop opened at sunrise.\n\nWe went downstairs together.\n\nThe first customer arrived before the doors were fully open. Then came another, and another. News of Iroh's return had traveled faster than the cart. People wanted stories, explanations, and reassurance that the world outside the walls had not changed beyond recognition.\n\nIroh offered tea.\n\nI offered the truth when I had it and silence when I did not.\n\nThe former soldier, Ren, arrived before noon. He stood outside until I opened the door for him.\n\n“You came back,” he said to Iroh.\n\n“I said I would.”\n\nRen looked at me. “You too.”\n\n“I said I would.”\n\nHe nodded as if the two promises belonged together.\n\n“The list helped,” Ren said. “Some families received word. Some did not. It was better than waiting without a name.”\n\nIroh's hands tightened around the cup he was holding.\n\n“Thank you for carrying it,” Ren continued.\n\n“You carried it first.”\n\nThe two men bowed to one another. Ren sat at the corner table and drank tea.\n\nBy afternoon, the shop had become crowded enough that I worked shoulder to shoulder with Iroh. Every time I reached for a cup, my fingers brushed. Every time he passed behind me, he warned me before coming close.\n\n“You are still asking,” I whispered.\n\n“I am still learning.”\n\n“You are allowed to become comfortable.”\n\n“Comfort does not replace respect.”\n\nThe answer made me look at him.\n\n“That is a good line,” I said.\n\n“You may write it down.”\n\n“I may steal it.”\n\n“Then I will charge you in pastries.”\n\nThe woman with the bright scarves returned near sunset. She looked at the two of us behind the counter and smiled.\n\n“I was wondering when you would stop pretending,” she said.\n\nIroh poured her tea. “We have never pretended.”\n\n“You have been discreet.”\n\n“There is a difference.”\n\nShe looked at me. “Are you happy?”\n\nThe question was direct enough to feel kind.\n\nI looked at Iroh. He did not answer for me.\n\n“Yes,” I said.\n\n“Good.” She lifted her cup. “Then the city may gossip if it needs exercise.”\n\nIroh's smile widened.\n\nAfter closing, I found a stack of letters on the counter. They were from people Iroh had met on the road. A governor asking for clarification. A village elder sending dried herbs. A child’s drawing of a dragon drinking tea.\n\n“You have brought the road home,” I said.\n\n“It followed me.”\n\n“Are you going to answer every letter?”\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“You need rest.”\n\n“I will rest after—”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe stopped.\n\n“You are allowed to be a person before you are a resource,” I said.\n\nIroh looked at me with a mixture of surprise and pride.\n\n“You are becoming very authoritative.”\n\n“I have a chair behind the counter.”\n\n“A dangerous position.”\n\n“Sit.”\n\nHe sat.\n\nI poured tea. Then I sat beside him instead of returning to work.\n\nFor a while, the two of us answered no letters.\n\nIroh leaned his head against my shoulder.\n\n“I missed this,” he said.\n\n“The chair?”\n\n“The silence.”\n\nI turned my face toward his hair. “I missed you.”\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n“I love you,” he said.\n\n“I love you too.”\n\nThe shop was closed. The city moved outside. The letters could wait until morning.\n\nI kissed him beneath the warm lights, and the distance of the road fell away.\n\nThe next morning, Iroh found the letters from the journey spread across the counter. I had sorted them into three piles: urgent, important, and needs tea before answering.\n\n“The third pile is the largest,” he said.\n\n“It is the most honest.”\n\n“You have created a system.”\n\n“I have created a boundary.”\n\nHe touched the edge of the pile. “I approve.”\n\n“You are not allowed to answer all of them today.”\n\n“I was not planning to.”\n\n“You were looking at the urgent pile.”\n\n“It was looking at me.”\n\n“Then both of you need rest.”\n\nIroh sat. I poured two cups and moved the letters aside.\n\n“Tell me about the road,” he said.\n\nI told him about the flower valley, the broken latch, the storm, and the child who asked whether he was a dragon. He laughed at the right places and went quiet at the others.\n\n“You made tea for strangers,” he said.\n\n“You taught me.”\n\n“You improved the lesson.”\n\n“Do not become jealous.”\n\n“I am very proud.”\n\nThe shop door opened. A customer waited outside with a tired child. Iroh stood, but I caught his sleeve.\n\n“Ask,” I said.\n\nHe looked at me, then nodded. “May I?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe opened the door.\n\nThe day began again, not as a return to the old routine, but as a continuation of the one we had built together.\n\nAt the end of the day, Iroh closed the door beside me. The lock clicked. His fingers found mine in the dark.\n\n“Home?” he asked.\n\nI looked up at the room above the shop.\n\n“Home,” I said.\n\nIroh smiled before he answered. “Then let us go upstairs.” We climbed together, carrying the day with us and leaving the letters below.\n\nThe room above the shop waited with clean blankets and a balcony full of evening air.\n\nThe tea cooled on the counter.\n\nThe stairs creaked. Iroh laughed softly when I complained about the third step.\n\n“It has been repaired,” he said.\n\n“It still complains.”\n\n“It has character.”\n\n“It learned from you.”\n\nIroh kissed my forehead before opening the bedroom door.\n\nIroh's smile answered before his voice did.\n\n“Then let us go upstairs.”\n\nWe climbed together, carrying the day with us and leaving the letters below.","word_count":1715},"avatar-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Things Iroh Cannot Carry","subtitle":"Jasmine After Rain · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"The bed shook once beneath me.\n\nNot from an earthquake. Iroh had sat upright so quickly that the mattress shifted against the wall. The room above the Jasmine Dragon was dark, and his hand had closed around the blanket until the cloth twisted like a rope.\n\n\"Iroh?\"\n\nHe did not answer.\n\nRain tapped the roof tiles. Down below, a cart rolled past the shuttered shop, its wheels catching on the same loose paving stone I had been meaning to report. An ordinary night in Ba Sing Se. I said the city aloud, slowly.\n\n\"We're upstairs. It's raining. There's a cart outside.\"\n\nHis breathing changed.\n\nHe looked toward the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\n\"Here.\"\n\n\"Forgive me. I must have disturbed you.\"\n\n\"You did wake me.\" I pulled the blanket back over my knees. \"That's different from asking for forgiveness.\"\n\nI could see his expression only when the street lantern caught the side of his face. He looked tired in a way that didn't belong to one bad dream.\n\n\"Would you like a light?\" I asked.\n\n\"A small one.\"\n\nI lit the lamp near the window. I didn't touch him, though I wanted to. His fingers were still curled against the blanket.\n\n\"Do you want me close?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not suddenly.\"\n\nI moved beside him and rested my hand open between us. After a moment he took it.\n\nThe quiet stretched. He wasn't going to tell me what he'd dreamed, and I didn't want to turn the silence into an interrogation.\n\nThen he said, \"In the dream, I knew where the men were going. I kept sending them anyway.\"\n\nI thought of the siege he rarely described and the grief that had followed him long after the fighting stopped.\n\n\"You don't have to finish that sentence.\"\n\n\"I know. I wish knowing it made the memory less loud.\"\n\nHe swallowed.\n\n\"I am very good at telling other people to rest. I am considerably less talented at obeying myself.\"\n\n\"That sounds familiar.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You?\"\n\n\"I can organize a whole square after a flood, then forget to eat until someone points at a bowl.\"\n\n\"An appalling habit.\"\n\n\"Yours or mine?\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nHe laughed once, without much joy.\n\nI asked whether he wanted tea. He said no; he didn't want to go downstairs and start treating the night like another shift. We stayed where we were until his hand relaxed.\n\n\"Would it help to speak to someone about the nightmares?\" I asked eventually.\n\nHe looked toward the lamp.\n\n\"Someone outside this room?\"\n\n\"Someone you choose. It doesn't have to be me.\"\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n\"Are you tired of listening?\"\n\nI was hurt by the question, then felt ashamed of being hurt.\n\n\"No. I'm saying I can love you and still not know how to help with every part of what you carry. I don't want my being here to become the only thing between you and a night like this.\"\n\nHe considered that. There was no comfortable way to tell someone I loved that I wasn't a cure, but I wanted him to believe I wasn't leaving because I said it.\n\n\"I know a healer who works with veterans,\" he said finally. \"She has written to me before.\"\n\n\"Would you like to write back?\"\n\n\"Perhaps in the morning.\"\n\n\"Then in the morning.\"\n\nHe set the lamp lower. I rested against him only after he moved his arm to make room. We did not go back to sleep quickly.\n\nAt dawn, Mina knocked on the downstairs door. Someone had arrived early with a delivery and needed the dry-goods store opened.\n\nIroh started to rise.\n\n\"I can get it,\" I said. \"You're allowed to stay upstairs.\"\n\n\"Are you certain?\"\n\n\"I can open a door and sign a receipt. I've had extensive training.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nMina and I checked the jars, rejected one cracked lid, and recorded the shortage. She didn't ask about our night. I didn't offer it.\n\nWhen I returned with breakfast, Iroh had placed a clean sheet of paper beside his cup.\n\n\"I have written a letter,\" he said.\n\n\"To the healer?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"I have not told her the dream. Only that I would like an appointment and that I prefer to speak privately.\"\n\n\"That sounds like enough to begin.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\nWe took the letter to the post, then walked slowly along the sheltered market street.\n\nBefore we left, he held the sealed envelope up to the light. The address was written in the fine, exact hand he used for messages that once carried orders to other men.\n\n“I have put my own name on it,” he said.\n\n“That seems like a good idea. Otherwise the healer might think you've sent her a recipe.”\n\n“No. I mean I haven't written ‘General.’” His smile was brief. “It felt odd to leave the title off.”\n\nI watched him fold the flap down one more time, though it was already closed. The old name had given him authority to send people where they might not return. Now he was asking someone to sit and listen, for a fee he would pay himself, at a time she might choose.\n\n“Does it feel wrong?” I asked.\n\n“Unfamiliar.” He turned the envelope over. “I do not know which stories I will be able to tell.”\n\n“Then you can say that.”\n\n“Do you know how many briefings I have given to soldiers who were terrified to admit they did not know what to say?”\n\n“More than I want to imagine.”\n\n“Quite.” He rubbed a thumb across the seal. “It seems I must take my own advice on a subject I am not qualified to command.”\n\nMina knocked gently at the open door with our breakfast. She didn't ask about the letter. She set down the bowl, told us the first customer had arrived, and said she could hold the counter for another fifteen minutes if we needed them.\n\nIroh looked at her. “Thank you. We have an errand.”\n\n“Then go,” she said. “The tea will be there when you come back.”\n\nFor once he believed her. I followed him into the street, the letter tucked inside his coat like something both fragile and entirely his.\n\nThe post clerk glanced at the address and told Iroh that a courier could collect the reply directly from the shop next time. He thanked her, then asked whether that meant the letter would arrive sooner. She explained the schedules and, with admirable patience, corrected his assumption about the mountain route.\n\nOutside, a man pushing a handcart struggled to get past a pile of empty crates. Iroh reached forward as though he might lift the entire thing himself.\n\n\"Should we ask?\" I said.\n\nHe nodded and approached.\n\n\"Would another pair of hands help?\"\n\nThe man shook his head. \"I need the shopkeeper to move the crates. If you lift the cart, it'll only block the lane again.\"\n\nIroh looked at the crates, then at me.\n\n\"You are enjoying this,\" he said.\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nWe found the shopkeeper and explained the obstruction without making it a crime. The crates were moved. The cart passed on its own wheels.\n\nThere were days when helping meant doing less than the first heroic impulse suggested.\n\nIroh watched the man disappear around the corner.\n\n\"That has become a theme in my life.\"\n\n\"I think it's a good one.\"\n\nHe turned his face toward the breeze and allowed himself a small, tired smile.\n\nI knew the nightmares weren't a problem a clear street could solve. But the morning had room for a difficult letter, a useful question, and the choice not to take charge of another person's cart. Rain had washed the stones dark. Iroh offered his arm, and I took it.\n\n\"I hope you do not think my weakness changes how I feel about you,\" he said.\n\n\"I don't think fear makes you weak.\"\n\n\"You have said that before.\"\n\n\"And you've listened before. We're both allowed to need another reminder.\"\n\nAt the corner, the baker was opening her shutters. She waved. Iroh waved back, a little shyly.\n\nWe had told each other about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory long before that morning. He knew I would cross to Lanternweald again, and that I loved other people. None of that changed how seriously I took what he'd trusted me with. I didn't promise to be the only person he could ask for help. I promised to remain truthful about when I could be here.\n\nBack in the courtyard, he set out two cups. The first tea was too weak.\n\n\"Terrible,\" I said.\n\n\"An offense against civilization.\"\n\n\"Shall we try again?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe made a second pot.\n\nIn the afternoon, a delivery boy arrived with a torn dispatch pouch and a list of streets he had been told to avoid. Somebody had circulated a rumor that the old war tunnels beneath the district were reopening. He wanted Iroh to tell him whether the rumor was true.\n\nThe look that passed over Iroh's face made my stomach tighten. For a second he was back in a command tent, sorting routes before anyone else could say they were afraid.\n\n“I cannot tell you about tunnels I haven't inspected,” he said.\n\n“But you know the city.”\n\n“I know a great many versions of the city.”\n\nThe boy twisted the strap of his pouch. “Then who do I ask?”\n\nIroh took out a sheet of shop paper and wrote down the district works office, the posted road closures, and the public notice board. He did not draw a secret route or offer to become the keeper of everybody's movements.\n\n“If they cannot answer, come back,” he said. “Not so I can promise safety. So I can help you ask again.”\n\nThe boy left with the paper tucked inside his coat.\n\n“That cost you,” I said after the bell stopped ringing.\n\nIroh lowered himself into the chair beside the stove. “I would have preferred to know everything.”\n\n“Me too.”\n\n“The dream made it difficult to remember that knowledge has boundaries.”\n\nI crouched to pick up the dispatch strap he'd dropped. I didn't touch his shoulder until he nodded. The kettle was cold, and the kitchen smelled faintly of yesterday's citrus peel.\n\nHe asked Mina to cover the counter for ten minutes. She checked the schedule, agreed to five, and brought him a cup of hot water without asking why he needed it. He thanked her and counted the minutes himself.\n\nThere was no victory in that afternoon. There was a rumor, a boy trying to get home, and a man who had chosen not to disguise uncertainty as authority. When Iroh stood up again, he took the empty cup back to the sink.\n\n\nThat evening he asked me to sit beside him while he read the healer's reply. I did. It offered a private appointment the following week. He wrote the date in his own calendar instead of asking me to manage it for him.\n\nLater he kissed me softly, his fingers still warm from the cup. I kissed him back, and we closed the bedroom door on a night that belonged to us, not the war.\n\nThe nightmares had not vanished. But there was another name in the notebook, another kind of help, and a morning waiting after the dark.","word_count":1876},"avatar-42":{"number":42,"title":"Six Tables and One Teapot","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"The trouble with the Six Tables feast was that there were only five usable tables.\n\nThe sixth had a loose leg.\n\nMara discovered the problem while placing bread on it. One basket leaned. The other followed. She caught both, but not before declaring the furniture an insult to bakers everywhere.\n\n\"I made that table,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Then you know who to complain to.\"\n\nIroh set down his teapot.\n\n\"Perhaps we could put it near the wall and reserve it for cups.\"\n\nMara pointed at the wobble.\n\n\"Do you want the tea in the cups or on the floor?\"\n\nI rubbed the bridge of my nose.\n\nThe feast was supposed to be simple. A community meal on Lanternwell Square, free to attend, with food brought by anyone who wished and enough seating for people who needed it. Greg had agreed to play music. Katara had arranged for two clinic volunteers to help serve. Neri had promised not to count how many guests arrived because the new privacy protocol made that an increasingly unpopular habit.\n\nIroh drew six rectangles in a dusting of spilled flour. \"If the serving pots stand here, the queue blocks the clear route. We must move them.\"\n\n\"Onto the cables?\" Greg called from his amplifier. \"Because I'm pretty sure the electrician has feelings about that.\"\n\nIroh shifted the imaginary pots. \"A sound objection. You understand your battlefield.\"\n\n\"Mostly I understand extension cords. Lost a gig to one once.\"\n\nTamsin arrived with a measuring tape. She marked an actual safe aisle and told them both to move their equipment until the route was clear, not merely clear from where they stood.\n\nMina counted bowls. Mara counted loaves. Nobody had enough volunteers to wash everything afterward.\n\n\"Then six tables present a seventh problem,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"That's not a strategy,\" Mara told him. \"That's dishes.\"\n\nHe rolled up his sleeves and carried two baskets without defending himself. Greg followed with a coil of cable, humming a tune that sounded suspiciously like a sound check performed backward.\n\nLater I watched them from the service line. Iroh thought in routes, ingredients, and people who had to be asked rather than commanded. Greg thought in rhythms, borrowed equipment, and getting a laugh when a microphone refused to cooperate. They were both kind. They were absolutely not the same man.\n\nThen everyone arrived at once.\n\nAang came through the Riverstone Door carrying three boxes of flatbread. Toph followed, announcing that the floor near the eastern arch was \"one good sneeze from embarrassment.\" Danse appeared with tools because somebody had mentioned a table. Greg brought two folding chairs and a guitar.\n\nMaui brought a banner.\n\nThe banner read SIX TABLES, ONE HEART.\n\nI stared at it.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"One heart?\"\n\n\"It's a metaphor.\"\n\n\"It's a terrible metaphor.\"\n\nHe looked wounded. \"It's festive.\"\n\n\"Some of us would like more than one heart involved in a community dinner.\"\n\nHe glanced toward Iroh, then at Greg, and finally understood why I was trying so hard not to laugh.\n\n\"Fine,\" he said. \"Six tables. Many hearts.\"\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nHe went to find fresh paint.\n\nIroh watched the exchange over the rim of his cup.\n\n\"Your patience is being tested.\"\n\n\"My patience left at breakfast.\"\n\n\"Then we will have to make tea without it.\"\n\nHe had agreed to serve one modest pot of the better leaves for the feast, while the pantry volunteers handled the larger kettles. It was a good plan. We had even written it down.\n\nThen a guest arrived carrying a jug of milk and asked whether anyone had considered a child with a dairy allergy.\n\nKatara was already checking the labels.\n\nGreg set his guitar aside and helped move the milk to a separate table. Toph tapped a foot against the stone and announced that the sixth table was not the only thing wobbling.\n\nBy noon, the square had become a collection of small problems, none catastrophic, all capable of making someone feel unwelcome.\n\nI found Pip near the fountain with a stack of place cards.\n\n\"Why are there names on the chairs?\" I asked.\n\n\"People thought it would be easier to seat everyone by the person they came with.\"\n\nI picked up a card bearing my name and saw three more cards beside it.\n\nMy stomach sank.\n\n\"Who wrote these?\"\n\n\"Someone from the welcome committee.\"\n\n\"Did they ask any of us?\"\n\nPip shook their head.\n\nI put the cards back in the stack.\n\n\"Then we won't use them.\"\n\nIt would have been easy to laugh. People were fascinated by who sat next to whom, as though a dinner arrangement could explain all the choices that made a relationship honest. But I had promised the people I cared about that our private agreements wouldn't become decorations for a public event.\n\nWe removed the cards.\n\nA few people complained. Iroh explained that the tables belonged to everyone, not to whichever gossip had reached the square first.\n\n\"Guests may sit wherever they are comfortable,\" he said. \"Or stand. Or eat at home. Hospitality is not an examination.\"\n\nI wanted to kiss him for that. Instead, I carried a tray.\n\nThe feast began late.\n\nGreg played a song about repairing chairs that made Tamsin threaten to throw a hammer into the canal. Katara directed the clinic volunteers with the focused patience of someone who had triaged considerably worse situations. Aang talked with two children about the difference between games and traditions that deserved context.\n\nMaui returned with a corrected banner.\n\nSIX TABLES, MANY HEARTS, PLEASE DO NOT SIT ON THE BROKEN ONE.\n\nIt was perfect.\n\nI almost told him so.\n\nThen I noticed the guest at the far edge of the square.\n\nThey were sitting on a low wall with an untouched bowl, their shoulders hunched. People passed in front of them without looking directly at their face. It was possible they wanted to be left alone. It was also possible they had nowhere to sit.\n\nI took a spare chair from the stack and stopped a few feet away.\n\n\"Would you like a chair?\" I asked.\n\n\"No, thank you.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI turned away.\n\n\"Wait,\" they said.\n\nI looked back.\n\n\"Does the meal cost anything?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Yes. People contribute if they can. People eat if they're hungry.\"\n\nThey studied the bowl in their hands.\n\n\"Someone told me I could help wash dishes.\"\n\n\"You can. But you don't have to earn your food.\"\n\nTheir face tightened in a way I recognized. Sometimes people had been given so much conditional kindness that an uncomplicated offer felt like a trap.\n\n\"I don't want to be counted,\" they said.\n\n\"Then we won't count you.\"\n\nI set the chair nearby, not close enough to imply they had accepted it. Then I walked back to the others.\n\nWhen I returned later with fresh water, they were sitting in it.\n\nIroh noticed the change.\n\nHe came beside me as the first lanterns lit above us.\n\n\"You did not try to persuade them.\"\n\n\"They said no.\"\n\n\"And then they found their way to yes.\"\n\n\"Maybe. Or maybe they just wanted a chair.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nAcross the square, Greg was teaching Maui a chorus. Maui sang far too loudly. Katara laughed when Toph demanded a better rhythm. Danse and Tamsin had finally repaired the sixth table, and Mara was inspecting it with the suspicion of someone who had been betrayed by furniture before.\n\nBy the time the bread ran low, people had begun volunteering things the feast didn't need. A visiting merchant offered a brass serving bell in exchange for having his name announced. Maui considered giving a speech. Greg considered giving the bell a drum solo. Mara considered locking the pantry.\n\n“Food first,” she said. “Then furniture. Then anybody who wants a solo can perform it outside.”\n\nGreg held up both hands. “I was mostly kidding.”\n\n“Mostly is doing a lot of work,” I told him.\n\nIroh caught my eye. “We may have a problem with the number of people asking for seconds.”\n\n“Do we have enough?”\n\n“We have enough for the children and the workers who haven't eaten yet. After that, no.”\n\nHe didn't make the shortage disappear. He asked Mara which portions she had reserved for volunteers, then checked the counts with her instead of inventing a more generous number. When a visitor complained, Iroh explained the limit in the same patient voice he'd used to greet him. There was an edge beneath the courtesy that reminded me of a commander holding a line.\n\nGreg set the guitar aside to help carry water, and Maui was given the very unglamorous task of folding chairs. He tried to bow while holding three at once. Toph told him that if he dropped one, he would have to listen to her describe the sound for a week.\n\nDanse inspected the fixed table with Tamsin and signed the repair sheet. Their signatures mattered more than the banner. Six places at a table weren't six invitations if somebody could lose their seat to a loose screw.\n\nMara's last basket went to the kitchen volunteers. A couple from the far district brought their own food and chose to eat near the open arch instead of joining the circle. No one chased them with an invitation.\n\nI was glad the feast wasn't perfect. The ledger would have a real total, the music would end when people were tired, and we would all have to return the borrowed chairs. That felt like a celebration inhabited by people rather than a painting of one.\n\nIroh reached for my hand beneath the edge of the serving cloth.\n\n\"You have been running all day,\" he said.\n\n\"So have you.\"\n\n\"Perhaps we should set a poor example and rest.\"\n\n\"I could be convinced.\"\n\nHe poured the last two cups from the special pot. The tea was faintly sweet. I took mine and sat beside him on the steps, watching the celebration continue without us.\n\nA gentle breeze lifted the hem of my sleeve. Iroh brushed a crumb from the edge of my mouth with his thumb, then waited.\n\n\"You have an audience,\" I murmured.\n\n\"Then they will have to practice looking elsewhere.\"\n\nI leaned into his kiss. It was warm and soft and lasted long enough for the music to become background noise.\n\nWhen we parted, I heard a small cough.\n\nMara stood behind us with a tray.\n\n\"If you two are finished being disgustingly sweet, somebody has to put the cups away.\"\n\nIroh accepted the tray with perfect dignity.\n\n\"I am told love involves service.\"\n\n\"Love can carry the dishes,\" Mara said.\n\nSo we did.\n\nBy the end of the evening, all six tables were occupied, nobody had been forced into a seat, and the quiet guest had gone home with a loaf of bread they hadn't needed to earn.\n\nFor a festival advertised with so much optimism, that seemed like a reasonable success.","word_count":1814},"avatar-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Dragon and the Demigod","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"Maui had a plan involving a wall of fire.\n\nI knew because he spread his arms across the Jasmine Dragon's counter and described it in tremendous detail while Iroh continued pouring tea with the expression of someone who had already heard three worse plans before breakfast.\n\n\"Imagine the lanterns,\" Maui said. \"All lighting at once. A giant flame across the terraces, then the whole sky—\"\n\n\"No,\" I said.\n\n\"I haven't finished.\"\n\n\"That's why I'm saying it now.\"\n\nIroh set down the kettle.\n\n\"Perhaps we should begin with what the festival requires.\"\n\n\"Joy,\" Maui declared.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wonder.\"\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"A little fire.\"\n\n\"Some,\" Iroh said, \"would be permissible in a properly contained lamp.\"\n\nMaui looked betrayed.\n\nWe were preparing the closing evening of the Longlight season, when residents hung the last of the wind-marker kites and lit low-smoke lanterns along the safe route. The storm had damaged several garden steps, and Tamsin had posted a clear fire and load plan. Open flame was permitted only inside protected lantern housings away from dry cloth and the old timber supports.\n\nMaui had apparently seen the word permitted and taken it as an artistic challenge.\n\n\"People like spectacle,\" he said.\n\n\"People also like having roofs,\" I answered.\n\nIroh smiled.\n\n\"Both are worth considering.\"\n\nWe took the plan to Tamsin, who was checking a repaired gutter outside the teahouse. She listened for exactly forty seconds before taking the paper and circling three places where flame would be too close to cloth, one where the walkway was too narrow, and another where heat could damage a wooden railing.\n\n\"That,\" she said, handing it back, \"is a list of things not to do.\"\n\nMaui frowned.\n\n\"You're all very difficult.\"\n\n\"We are trying to remain alive,\" I said.\n\nIroh looked at the drawings.\n\n\"What is it you want people to feel when the lanterns are lit?\" he asked.\n\nMaui hesitated. Then he spread his arms as though he could fit the entire village between them.\n\n\"I want them to look up! I want them to say, 'Whoa, did the great Maui do that?'\" He caught Iroh's expression. \"Fine, yes, and I want the kids who hauled those boards to see this place can still be spectacular. Is it a crime to want both?\"\n\nIroh's expression softened.\n\n\"Then let us make something memorable that doesn't ask anyone to stand beneath falling fire.\"\n\nWe moved the planning meeting to the garden.\n\nIroh brought a box of shallow metal trays, polished on the inside. He set one beside a lantern and angled it toward the wall. The small flame reflected across the curved surface, making a broad patch of warm light.\n\nMaui leaned closer.\n\n\"That's clever.\"\n\n\"It is a simple trick of light,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Still clever.\"\n\nI took another tray and tried the angle. My first attempt sent the reflection directly into Iroh's eyes.\n\nHe blinked.\n\n\"Perhaps a little lower.\"\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\nMaui laughed.\n\nWe spent the afternoon testing the arrangement. With six lanterns and six reflectors, we could illuminate the old stone wall without adding more heat than the fire plan allowed. The metal would cool before being packed away. The accessible route remained clear.\n\nPip suggested small carved shapes in front of the reflectors, so the light made moving shadows on the wall when the breeze reached the lantern housings.\n\nMaui drew a fish.\n\nI drew a star.\n\nIroh drew a very round teapot.\n\n\"That is a dangerous creature,\" Maui said solemnly.\n\n\"It has defeated many impatient men,\" Iroh replied.\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt dusk, the volunteers tested the display. The light spread across the stone in overlapping gold shapes. A fish swam slowly through the shadow of a star. The teapot appeared to steam, though the effect was only light moving through a little paper cutout.\n\nChildren gathered beneath the shelter and pointed.\n\nMaui watched their faces.\n\nHe looked almost as pleased as he would have if the sky had caught fire.\n\n\"Better than the giant flame?\" I whispered.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Different.\"\n\n\"Safer.\"\n\n\"Very different.\"\n\nIroh stepped beside him.\n\n\"One does not have to become smaller to give someone else room to see.\"\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"Was that wisdom?\"\n\n\"Only an observation.\"\n\n\"You're suspiciously good at those.\"\n\nIroh smiled.\n\nThe argument moved outside while Pip checked the screens. Maui leaned against the courtyard wall and looked at Iroh as though the older man had committed a personal insult.\n\n\"You're really going to light the whole thing with ordinary lamps?\"\n\n\"They are lamps with an excellent history of lighting things,\" Iroh replied.\n\n\"Where's the spectacle?\"\n\n\"In the shadow someone wishes to show.\"\n\nMaui made a dissatisfied noise. I was about to intervene when Iroh held up a hand—not to silence either of us, but to ask for a moment.\n\n\"Tell me something, Maui. When you learned to pull an island from the sea, did you know who would live there?\"\n\nMaui stared at him. His tattoos shifted over his arm. \"Not everyone.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps a lantern belongs to whoever uses its light, not to whoever can make the brightest flame.\"\n\nI winced. It was sharper than Iroh usually sounded. Maui heard it too.\n\n\"That's a pretty way to tell me to shut up.\"\n\nIroh closed his eyes for a moment. \"It was a pretty way to avoid apologizing for having been rude.\"\n\nI looked between them. That admission mattered more than the proverb.\n\nMaui rubbed the back of his neck. \"I wasn't listening to Pip. I heard a festival and imagined a crowd chanting my name.\"\n\n\"An understandable temptation,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Oh, you want applause too?\"\n\n\"Certainly. When the jasmine cakes rise properly, I am insufferable.\"\n\nMaui laughed so loudly that someone inside asked whether the screen frame had fallen.\n\nI took a step toward him, then stopped. I loved both men. I'd told each of them plainly that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory; I didn't want that to make me an interpreter for every conflict they chose to have.\n\n\"Work it out between you,\" I said. \"Pip still needs the lamps.\"\n\nMaui held out his hand to Iroh. \"I'll carry them.\"\n\nIroh took it. \"I'll ask where they should go.\"\n\nThey went inside without asking me to decide who had been wiser. I stood in the courtyard for another breath, smelling hot paper and tea, and felt the strange relief of not being responsible for making two people I loved agree.\n\nBefore the public was invited inside, Pip asked us to test the screens once more. A paper edge had curled near a candle. Nobody had been hurt, but the demonstration wasn't going to open while somebody could brush a hand against hot paper.\n\nMaui opened his mouth. I could almost see his argument assembling itself: he could move fire safely, he had faced the sea, he had personally solved problems much larger than this.\n\nIroh put a hand on his arm. \"Let them finish.\"\n\nMaui looked betrayed. Then he glanced at the child standing beside the frame, clutching a paper boat they'd made.\n\n\"Will it be all right?\" the child asked.\n\n\"After we move the lamp,\" Pip answered. \"If you'd like, you can help choose where your boat's shadow goes.\"\n\n\"Only for one song.\"\n\n\"Then only for one song.\"\n\nWe adjusted the frame and waited for it to cool. Maui held the little boat as carefully as if it were made of glass. He asked the child whether the shadow looked right. The child shook their head, so he moved it to the left.\n\n\"Better?\"\n\n\"Perfect.\"\n\nI laughed at the pride on his face. Iroh leaned close enough to speak quietly.\n\n\"You chose well when you told him the sky did not need catching fire,\" he said.\n\n\"I chose self-preservation. We'd still be arguing about insurance.\"\n\nHe chuckled.\n\nA vendor came to ask whether we wanted more lantern oil. Pip checked the inventory before agreeing to buy enough for the scheduled display. Nobody was being asked to donate supplies they hadn't promised. The little festival felt less magical for all its lists and receipts, but it was also less likely to leave someone cleaning up alone at midnight.\n\nIroh touched my hand. \"Will you stay beside me for the opening?\"\n\n\"Yes. Afterward I'm helping Maui and the children put away the reflectors.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't mind?\"\n\n\"I may wish I had more time. That is different from claiming yours.\"\n\nI smiled at him. We'd spoken openly about my being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory. Our relationships were chosen, not ranked, and neither man had to pretend to feel nothing about sharing time.\n\n\"Then I'll find you when the last lantern goes out,\" I said.\n\n\"With tea?\"\n\n\"With tea.\"\n\nHe glanced at my mouth, then asked if he might kiss me before the crowd arrived. I stepped closer. His kiss was slow and warm, with the faint taste of jasmine that always seemed to linger on his lips.\n\nWhen we parted, Maui was balancing the child's boat on one finger.\n\n\"Don't,\" I called.\n\n\"I wasn't!\"\n\nThe obvious lie made us all laugh, including the child. The little boat survived the evening, and so did the paper screens.\n\nAt rehearsal, Maui lifted a polished reflector so high that its beam flashed straight into a child's eyes.\n\n“Put it down,” Iroh said.\n\nHis voice was quiet and absolute. Maui obeyed before remembering he had not agreed to take orders.\n\n“Hey! I know how to hold a piece of metal.”\n\n“And the child knows how it feels to be dazzled by it,” Iroh replied.\n\nTamsin turned the reflector toward the ground while Pip checked on the child. I watched Iroh realize how he had sounded. The Dragon of the West had arrived in the middle of a festival rehearsal, and not everybody was pleased to meet him.\n\n“You don't command my hands,” Maui said.\n\n“No,” Iroh answered. “The danger was real. The command was my mistake.”\n\nMaui looked at the reflector and at Mini Maui, who had covered his tattooed face with both hands. “Okay. New plan. I carry it lower. The great Maui is capable of learning a thing or two.”\n\n“Remarkable,” I murmured.\n\n“I heard that!”\n\nIroh asked Tamsin to determine the safe angles rather than rearranging the display himself. Maui allowed a nine-year-old to choose the paper fish's position, although he announced the choice as though the child had just received an award.\n\nThe two men weren't interchangeable: Maui wanted the sky to notice; Iroh wanted to know who could get out if the lamps failed. Their better ideas came only after they let the people running the festival decide what was needed.\n\n\nThe display opened that evening. The first visitor asked how the shadows worked. Pip explained the reflectors. Another asked whether the light was magic. Iroh showed them the metal tray and let them examine it after it cooled.\n\nMaui stayed at the side of the path, helping children place their own paper shapes at a safe distance from the lanterns.\n\nNo one needed to be dazzled into gratitude.\n\nA child with a paper dragon approached while we were putting away the last screen. The painted wings had begun to curl from the damp, and the child was clutching it so hard the tail had crumpled.\n\n“Is mine too small?” they asked Maui.\n\nHe looked from the dragon to the fish reflector he'd carried across the courtyard all afternoon. I knew that expression. He could make a dragon large enough to throw a shadow across the whole street, and some part of him wanted to do it immediately.\n\n“Too small for what?” Iroh asked.\n\n“To be seen.”\n\nIroh crouched to their level, careful not to put his own hand over the paper. “Where would you like its shadow to appear?”\n\nThe child pointed at the wall beside the bench. Maui examined the angle, then moved the reflector stand a little farther back. Pip came to check the clearance and asked them to use the cooler lantern rather than the candle closest to the paper.\n\nI held the screen while the child unfolded the dragon's tail. When the lamp was lit, the shadow stretched across the plaster and looked almost enormous. The child squealed.\n\nMaui drew himself up. “Well. A dragon of considerable distinction.”\n\n“It looks like mine,” the child said.\n\n“Of course it does,” Iroh answered.\n\nLater, when the family collected the paper dragon, Maui tried to explain to me why the little shadow had been better than his first plan. His hands worked furiously in the air. He had no word for it that didn't sound like surrender.\n\n“You can still make beautiful things,” I said. “You just don't need to make somebody else's dragon into your monument.”\n\nHe blinked. “I was going to say the fish looked better.”\n\n“Then the fish looked better.”\n\nIroh's laugh reached us from the doorway. It was low and delighted, not the practiced voice of a man teaching a lesson. Maui spun toward him.\n\n“You're on my side, General?”\n\n“I am on the side of an excellent fish,” Iroh said, lifting a tray out of the path.\n\nAfter the last lantern was extinguished, Iroh and I cleaned the trays. His sleeves were rolled to the elbow, and there was a little soot on his wrist.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" I said.\n\n\"I am enjoying the silence after so many questions.\"\n\n\"Did you mind working with Maui?\"\n\n\"Not at all.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"He reminds me of people who once believed greatness required everyone to be looking at them.\"\n\nI tilted my head.\n\n\"People you knew?\"\n\n\"One or two.\"\n\n\"One of them you?\"\n\nHis smile turned rueful.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\nI dried the last tray.\n\n\"I love him,\" I said. \"And I love you. I'm glad you can share a project without having to decide what that means for your relationship with each other.\"\n\nIroh took my hand.\n\n\"We are not required to be the same kind of companion to care for the same person.\"\n\nThe words warmed me more than the lanterns had.\n\nHe kissed my fingers, then waited while I moved closer.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHis kiss was gentle and a little amused, as though he'd found another secret worth keeping. The kitchen smelled of tea and soot, and the last lamp glowed beside the door.\n\nThe next morning, Maui delivered the reflectors back to storage with a handwritten note.\n\nTHANK YOU FOR LETTING THE FISH BE HEROIC.\n\nIroh pinned it above the worktable.\n\nFor weeks afterward, it made him smile.","word_count":2413},"avatar-05":{"number":5,"title":"The City's Quiet Scars","subtitle":"Jasmine After Rain · Chapter 5 of 40","content":"Ba Sing Se had survived the war, but the city did not pretend survival meant healing.\n\nI noticed it in the pauses. People stopped talking when soldiers passed. Older residents watched the palace walls with expressions that held more history than trust. Some streets still carried scorch marks beneath fresh paint. In the market, a child asked his mother why the soldiers were wearing different colors now.\n\nShe answered softly. The child did not look convinced.\n\nThe Jasmine Dragon sat near a street where the scars were harder to hide. The wall across from the shop had been repaired, but the bricks were a slightly different shade. Iroh had planted jasmine along the base. New leaves climbed over old damage.\n\n“You keep trying to make the wall pretty,” I said.\n\n“Pretty is not the same as healed,” he replied. “But it can be a beginning.”\n\nI stood beside him with a watering jar. The morning was cool. Iroh wore a gray robe and carried a pair of shears.\n\n“Do you ever want to leave?” I asked.\n\nHe clipped a dead branch. “Sometimes.”\n\n“Why stay?”\n\n“Because leaving can be necessary. It can also be an excuse.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For avoiding the places where our presence is inconvenient.”\n\nI looked at the damaged wall. “Are you inconvenient here?”\n\n“To some people.”\n\n“Because of who you were?”\n\n“Because of what I did. Because of what I represented. Because people are allowed to remember the harm caused by a nation even after its leaders change.”\n\nThe shears closed with a small click.\n\n“Do you think they should forgive you?”\n\nIroh's face remained calm, but his eyes grew distant. “Forgiveness is a gift. I am not entitled to it.”\n\n“What are you entitled to?”\n\n“The chance to act differently.”\n\nThe answer stayed with me while the shop opened. I served tea to a woman who had lost her home. I listened to a man complain about the new government. I handed a child a sweet bun and watched him smile despite the careful way his mother held his hand.\n\nIroh moved among them with the same patience he offered everyone. But when a former soldier entered, he became very still.\n\nThe man was older, with a scar across his jaw and a stiff walk. He stared at Iroh for a long time.\n\n“General,” he said.\n\nThe title fell into the room like a dropped cup.\n\nIroh bowed his head. “You may call me Iroh.”\n\n“Can I?”\n\n“You may call me whatever you need to call me. I will answer to Iroh.”\n\nThe former soldier's mouth tightened. He ordered tea and sat near the door.\n\nI served him. His hands shook when he lifted the cup.\n\nIroh did not ask him to leave.\n\nAfter the man finished, he placed coins on the table and stood.\n\n“My brother died at Ba Sing Se,” he said.\n\nIroh's expression did not change, but the room seemed to draw breath around him.\n\n“I am sorry,” he said.\n\n“You should be.”\n\n“I am.”\n\nThe man left.\n\nI watched Iroh remain by the table.\n\n“Do you want me to say something?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Do you want me to leave?”\n\n“No.”\n\nI stood beside him. The tea cup still held a little steam.\n\n“That was not a fair thing for him to put on you,” I said.\n\nIroh looked down. “It was not unfair for him to say it.”\n\n“Both can be true.”\n\nHis gaze moved to my face. “Yes.”\n\nThe shop filled again. The moment was interrupted, but not erased.\n\nA potter arrived just as the afternoon light reached the repaired wall. She had brought a cup with a hairline crack and a bill for the glaze, and she looked at Iroh's robe before deciding to speak to me.\n\n\"The general's vines are growing over the maker's mark on those bricks,\" she said. \"My aunt fired them. She wants people to know who rebuilt the street.\"\n\nIroh stopped polishing a teapot. For a moment his expression was unmistakably that of a man rearranging a campaign in his head.\n\n\"Then we must not conceal her signature,\" he said.\n\n\"You could just cut back the jasmine,\" the potter replied. \"I wasn't asking for a speech.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"I was considering how to keep the stems from damaging her mortar.\"\n\nHe put down the cloth and came outside with us. I held the watering jar while he showed the potter where he hoped to train the vines. She examined the roots and corrected the placement of two supporting hooks. Iroh fetched a pencil, marked her changes, and asked whether her aunt would approve the work before he touched the wall.\n\nThe potter looked less angry when she left, though she had not forgiven anything. I did not pretend to know whether she ever would.\n\n\"You were about to move an entire garden,\" I told him.\n\n\"Only three climbing stems,\" he said.\n\n\"With the face of a man planning a siege.\"\n\n\"Gardening is a very serious art.\"\n\nWhen he brushed dirt from my wrist with his thumb, the touch surprised both of us. He withdrew gently, allowing me to smile without having to make it mean more than it did. In the shop the repaired cup remained on the counter, waiting for its maker to decide whether it could be used.\n\nAt closing, Iroh did not pour tea. He stood in the darkened room with both hands on the counter.\n\n“I have not told you about my son,” he said.\n\nI stayed near the door. “You don’t have to.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But you want to.”\n\n“I do.”\n\nHe told me about Lu Ten. Not every detail. Not the battlefield or the letter or the empty place at the table. He told me about a boy who laughed too loudly, who loved sweet tea, who wanted to be a better soldier than his father.\n\n“He died during the siege,” Iroh said. “I had believed victory would make the loss meaningful. It did not. It only made the silence larger.”\n\nI listened without interrupting.\n\n“I spent years believing grief was a debt I had to keep paying,” he continued. “If I was happy, I thought I was betraying him. If I rested, I thought I had not earned it.”\n\n“Do you still believe that?”\n\n“Some days.”\n\n“What do you do on those days?”\n\n“I make tea.”\n\nI smiled faintly. “That’s not a very complete answer.”\n\n“I also eat something. My nephew insists.”\n\n“Zuko?”\n\n“He is very persistent.”\n\n“Does he know about the tea shop?”\n\n“He knows too much about everything.”\n\nI stepped closer. “What do you want people to know about you?”\n\nIroh thought for a long time.\n\n“That I loved my son,” he said. “That I caused harm. That both facts remain true. That I am still learning how to live in the space between them.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\n“You don’t have to be punished forever to prove you’re sorry,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“But you do have to keep choosing what you do next.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe reached for my hand. His fingers were warm from the kettle.\n\n“You are becoming very wise,” he said.\n\n“I’m borrowing your lines.”\n\n“Then I am honored.”\n\nThe shop was dark except for the lamp over the counter. Iroh lifted my hand and kissed my knuckles.\n\n“Can I kiss you?” he asked.\n\n“You can.”\n\nHis mouth found mine. The kiss carried grief without being consumed by it. I touched his face, feeling the lines beneath my fingers. He leaned into my palm like a man who had forgotten he was allowed to be held.\n\nWhen I parted, he rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“You do not need to rescue me,” he whispered.\n\n“I’m not trying to.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“I’m trying to be here.”\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\n“That,” he said, “is more than enough.”\n\nOutside, the city moved through another night of rebuilding.\n\nInside, jasmine climbed the old wall.\n\nBefore opening the shop, I and Iroh sat on the front step with two cups of tea. The street was quiet. Morning light rested on the repaired bricks.\n\n“You stayed,” he said.\n\n“I work here.”\n\n“You stayed beyond the work.”\n\nI looked at the jasmine vines. “So did you.”\n\nIroh's smile was tired and grateful.\n\n“Perhaps that is what we are learning together,” he said. “How to remain without pretending the past did not happen.”\n\nI lifted my cup. “To remaining.”\n\nHe touched his cup to mine. “To choosing it.”\n\nThe shop bell rang behind me. The first customer had arrived.\n\nIroh stood and offered his hand.\n\nI took it, and the two of us went inside.\n\nThe former soldier's visit stayed with me through the morning. I watched every customer more carefully. A woman who sat alone and kept her back to the wall. A boy who flinched when someone dropped a tray. An old man who paid with coins worn nearly smooth.\n\nThe city was full of people carrying things nobody else could see.\n\nAt midday, I asked Iroh whether he ever wished he could set his memories down and leave them somewhere.\n\n“Often,” he said.\n\n“Where would you put them?”\n\n“In a room with a lock.”\n\n“Would you go back?”\n\n“Sometimes. To remember. Not to live there.”\n\nHe poured tea into two cups. “Memories are not houses. They are guests. Some are rude. Some are kind. None should be allowed to decide who may enter the future.”\n\n“You make it sound easy.”\n\n“It is not easy. It is practice.”\n\n“Like tea?”\n\n“Like tea. You pour, you taste, you adjust. You do not condemn the entire harvest because one cup is bitter.”\n\nI took a sip. The blend was perfect.\n\n“What if the cup is bitter because you made it that way?”\n\n“Then you learn what you did and brew another.”\n\nHis hand rested beside mine on the counter. I turned my palm upward. He covered it with his.\n\n“You cannot change the first cup,” he said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“But you can choose what you serve next.”\n\nThe shop door opened. Customers entered. Iroh let go of my hand, but the warmth remained.\n\nThat evening, after the last cup was washed, I went outside to water the jasmine. The wall still showed the faint shape of old fire beneath the leaves.\n\nIroh stood beside me.\n\n“You stayed,” he said again.\n\n“You keep noticing.”\n\n“I will continue.”\n\nI leaned against him. “Then keep noticing tomorrow.”\n\n“I will.”\n\nThe jasmine leaves trembled in the night breeze.\n\nIroh's shoulder rested against mine.\n\nFor a moment, neither of us spoke. The silence was not empty. It held the wall, the city, and the lives I was both still learning to carry.\n\nThe morning bell rang, and We went inside together.\n\nThe kettle answered from the kitchen.\n\nThe city was waiting too.\n\nI opened the door beside Iroh. The first light entered the shop, touching the repaired wall and the jasmine leaves.","word_count":1815},"avatar-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Shape of Forgiveness","subtitle":"Jasmine After Rain · Chapter 27 of 40","content":"The hill was steeper than I remembered.\n\nIroh had said we were going to see the view. He had packed tea, an offering bowl, and a folded cloth in the careful way he packed things when he didn't want to talk about them. I knew where we were going before we left the shop.\n\nThe wind was strong enough to pull loose strands of hair across my face. Near the top, Iroh stopped to let a small procession pass. A family carried flowers and a plain wooden box, speaking softly among themselves. They didn't recognize him, or perhaps they did and chose not to say anything.\n\n\"Should we wait?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe sat on a flat stone just off the path. Below us, Ba Sing Se went about its business: carts crossing the roads, workers calling to one another, kitchens starting their afternoon fires. It felt wrong and right that the city could be so alive while he was carrying grief up a hill.\n\nWhen the others had gone, we reached the memorial place. Iroh brushed the leaves away without hurrying. He set down the bowl and stood still.\n\nI didn't reach for his hand. I wanted to, badly. My wish to comfort him was not more important than his right to choose how this moment went.\n\nHe began talking about Lu Ten's laugh.\n\nNot the solemn version I had heard when other people spoke of his son. A ridiculous, helpless laugh. A laugh that once startled a messenger so much he dropped a stack of papers in front of the entire officers' tent.\n\n\"What made him laugh?\" I asked.\n\n\"Me,\" Iroh said, and looked almost offended by the memory. \"I was attempting to look important.\"\n\n\"That does sound funny.\"\n\n\"I was a very impressive general.\"\n\n\"Apparently not impressive enough.\"\n\nHe smiled. Then the smile broke apart.\n\n\"I cannot remember the exact sound anymore,\" he said.\n\nThe admission seemed to cost him more than the walk. I looked at the offering bowl because looking straight at him felt too much like expecting him to perform his grief.\n\n\"Do you want me to say something?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. Not yet.\"\n\nWe watched the grass bend. I thought of the stories people told about forgiveness, how neatly they arranged the moment when pain was supposed to stop hurting. Nothing neat happened on the hill. Iroh was a man who had made terrible choices in a terrible war, who had loved his child, and who could not undo either of those facts.\n\nHe sat down beside the bowl.\n\n\"Some mornings I wonder whether a better father would have been a worse general,\" he said. \"And whether being that father would have brought my son home.\"\n\nI had no answer. It was hard not to offer one anyway.\n\n\"That sounds like something nobody can prove,\" I said at last.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I don't want to tell you to forgive yourself because it would make this easier for me.\"\n\nHe closed his eyes briefly.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nA bird landed on a branch above us, shouted at nothing, and flew away. Iroh made a startled noise that was almost a laugh.\n\n\"Even the birds are impatient with my solemnity.\"\n\n\"I think it was offended by my tea.\"\n\n\"You brewed the tea?\"\n\n\"You left me the kettle.\"\n\n\"A dangerous delegation.\"\n\nIroh held the cup between his hands. For a while I thought he had no more words.\n\n\"When Lu Ten was young,\" he said at last, \"he wrote letters home from training. Most of them were astonishingly dull.\"\n\n\"Dull?\"\n\n\"Descriptions of weather. Food. Someone forgetting his boots. I kept every one.\"\n\nI looked at the memorial stone. \"I don't think that sounds dull.\"\n\n\"Neither did I. I liked knowing he had lived another ordinary day.\"\n\n\"Were there any you didn't understand?\" I asked.\n\n\"Many. He referred to people by nicknames I never learned. Once he told me a tent had been taken prisoner by the wind. I spent a full hour wondering which unit had lost its shelter.\"\n\n\"Did you ask?\"\n\n\"I wrote an excellent, very stern inquiry.\"\n\n\"Of course you did.\"\n\n\"He sent back a drawing of a tent with chains on its wrists.\"\n\nIroh smiled at his cup. I could picture his younger self, surrounded by maps, taking the time to argue with a drawing. The image made my throat tighten in a way I could not explain.\n\n\"Sometimes,\" he said, \"I fear that if I tell you the funny things, you will forget what the army did.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"Or that you will think I was always the person you know now.\"\n\n\"I won't do that either.\"\n\nHe turned the cup once, testing its warmth against his palms. The wind kept lifting the folded cloth at the edge of the offering bowl. I held it steady so he would not have to set the cup down.\n\n\"You can love him without telling me a clean story about yourself,\" I said.\n\n\"An inconvenient kindness,\" Iroh replied. \"You have learned from some very troublesome company.\"\n\nI waited until he offered a corner of the cloth. Then I sat beside him and helped keep it in place. The bowl, the letters, and his memories belonged to him; I could share the wind without claiming anything else.\n\nThe wind moved the flowers along the path. Below us a cart rattled through the street, its driver calling for someone to close a gate. Ba Sing Se was still full of people worrying about little things. Iroh watched the city with an expression I hadn't learned to name.\n\n\"His laugh was very loud,\" he said. \"Louder than mine, if you can imagine that.\"\n\n\"Unlikely.\"\n\n\"An outrageous judgment.\"\n\n\"He was your son. I assume he inherited the worst parts of your humor.\"\n\nIroh laughed, and then his eyes filled with tears. He covered them with his hand. I watched the clouds rather than making him perform grief where I could see it.\n\n\"I'm glad you told me,\" I said.\n\n\"It doesn't make a very grand story.\"\n\n\"I don't need a grand one.\"\n\nHe spoke again about a little wooden fish Lu Ten had brought home from a market. It had been badly carved, with one side larger than the other. Iroh had put it on a shelf until the war scattered everything he owned. Remembering it hurt because it had been part of a life with no reason to expect an ending.\n\nI wanted to say that memories couldn't be taken away, but that wasn't true either. People forgot things. Faces changed. Words disappeared. Sometimes a small object was simply gone.\n\n\"Would you like to draw it?\" I asked.\n\nHe considered. \"My drawing is terrible.\"\n\n\"So is mine.\"\n\nWe took out the notebook I carried and spent a few minutes arguing about the shape of a fish. Our drawings were awful. His had a tail that looked suspiciously like a teapot spout.\n\n\"Magnificent,\" I said.\n\n\"Clearly.\"\n\nI left the page with him. He folded it carefully and put it in his sleeve. It wasn't a substitute for the toy or the boy who had loved it. It was something he'd chosen to make today.\n\nWhen he offered his hand, I took it. He didn't ask me to solve his sorrow. I didn't ask him to make the hill into a monument to our romance. We stayed together until he was ready to walk down, and I let the rest of the afternoon be quiet.\n\nWe poured a little into the bowl and shared the rest. I told him about the first time I had tried to make a memorial into a task list, how I had wanted to be useful so badly that I nearly forgot why I was there. He listened without turning my confession into a lesson.\n\n\"I don't know where I belong in this part of your life,\" I admitted.\n\n\"Beside me, when you wish to be.\"\n\n\"Not as a replacement for anybody.\"\n\n\"Never.\"\n\nI took a breath. \"I've said before that I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. Loving more than one person doesn't make any of them interchangeable. I don't want to treat Lu Ten's memory that way either. Your grief has a place. So do I. Those aren't competing seats.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a long moment.\n\n\"You explain yourself in such complicated ways,\" he said.\n\n\"You're the one with an entire secret society.\"\n\n\"An unfair comparison.\"\n\nWe both laughed, quietly. He offered me his hand, and I accepted.\n\nOn the way down he stopped to help a young person whose basket had tipped on the slope. We gathered the spilled oranges. The person tried to pay him with one, and he argued cheerfully that the first orange had been payment enough. I bought two so they wouldn't lose the sale.\n\nBack at the Jasmine Dragon, Mina had kept the evening shift running. She passed Iroh a list of repairs rather than asking where he'd been. He thanked her. I put the kettle away.\n\nWhen the shutters were drawn, he asked whether I would sit with him upstairs for a little while. There was no grand declaration. He was tired. I was tired too.\n\nHe kissed me goodnight, gently and with no attempt to make the day's sadness disappear. I stayed until his breathing settled, then turned down the lamp. The hill remained part of his life. I had been allowed to visit it, not claim it.","word_count":1572},"avatar-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Price of the Mountain Mint","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass three","content":"The supplier's letter smelled of rain and spoiled leaves.\n\nIroh read it at the back counter, his expression growing more serious with every line.\n\n\"Bad news?\" I asked.\n\n\"The mountain harvest was poor. Too much rain at the wrong time.\"\n\nHe handed me the note.\n\nThe supplier had lost nearly half the expected mint crop. Transporting what remained would cost more, and the farmers had asked their regular buyers to take smaller orders rather than forcing prices low enough to leave families without income.\n\n\"They're trying to share the loss,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Can we manage with less?\"\n\nIroh looked toward the tea tins.\n\n\"We can. But not if I insist on serving everything as though nothing has changed.\"\n\nHe sounded unhappy about it.\n\nThe Jasmine Dragon had already lost stock in the roof leak. We needed another shipment, but the price was now nearly twice what we'd planned. The bakery was feeling the same shortage in some of its herbs. At the clinic, Katara's volunteers had been rationing mint for certain medicinal preparations.\n\nNone of this could be solved by wishing a more generous harvest into existence.\n\nWe called a meeting with the people who relied on the shipments.\n\nMara arrived with flour on her sleeve. Tamsin brought a pencil and the expression of somebody prepared to object to numbers that didn't add up. Neri came with the route schedule. Aang had sent a note from Riverstone asking us to prioritize the clinic's confirmed medicinal needs.\n\nIroh poured a simpler tea than usual.\n\nNobody complained.\n\n\"We can divide what remains,\" he said, \"but I will not ask the growers to sell at a loss.\"\n\nMara nodded.\n\n\"Good. They're people, not a cupboard.\"\n\nMina turned the supplier's note over and ruled three columns on the back: what had been ordered, what could be spared, and what somebody had actually promised to pay for.\n\n\"Before you negotiate,\" she said, \"tell us whose money is being used.\"\n\n\"Mine, if we must cover the shortfall,\" Iroh replied.\n\n\"Your share of the business, you mean. The apprentices' wages aren't a reserve fund.\"\n\nHe inclined his head. \"You are correct.\"\n\nTamsin checked the proposed carriage route against the rain damage. One road was quicker, but the wheels would cut through an untested embankment. Neri pointed to the longer road and said the carriers had asked for proper rest stops along it.\n\n\"We can ask the grower to split the shipment,\" Iroh said, drawing arrows across the margin. \"One sealed crate for the clinic, smaller tins for the other buyers. Nothing moves until the carrier confirms the price.\"\n\nMara frowned at the page. \"That will mean less mint in my bread.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Iroh said. \"And I shall have less in my finest tea.\"\n\n\"You're taking that very bravely.\"\n\n\"With considerable suffering.\"\n\nI laughed. The humor did not make the shortage smaller; it kept us from treating it as an opportunity to praise ourselves. The decision was still going to cost money and disappoint customers. Iroh counted the crates a second time rather than insisting that good intentions would somehow fit more leaves inside them.\n\nThe figures were less encouraging.\n\nThe clinic needed a guaranteed supply. The bakery could change two recipes. The teahouse could reduce its premium blend, but enough customers depended on the ordinary tea for a small comfort during their working day that raising every price would hurt people who had little to spare.\n\n\"Then don't raise every price,\" I said.\n\nTamsin lifted an eyebrow.\n\n\"Where does the money come from?\"\n\n\"Let's find out before we guess.\"\n\nWe listed the costs on one sheet: tea, fuel, clean water, staff time, transport, cup repairs, and an emergency reserve. Iroh watched me write.\n\n\"You're forgetting something,\" he said.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Payment for the growers' risk.\"\n\nI added it.\n\nThe numbers became uglier.\n\nMara smiled.\n\n\"At least they're honest.\"\n\nA good part of the morning went into arguing over what fair meant. Iroh wanted everyone to have the best tea he could serve. Tamsin wanted the business to survive. Neri wanted the delivery route kept open. I wanted us to stop talking as though these desires were enemies.\n\nIn the end, we designed three offerings.\n\nThe ordinary house blend would remain affordable. It used local herbs and roasted grain with a little citrus peel, warm and pleasant without pretending to be rare mountain mint. A smaller premium service would cost more and use the limited mint supply, with the price clearly explained. The clinic's confirmed allotment would be reserved before any tea was sold.\n\n\"No free miracle,\" I said.\n\n\"No,\" Iroh agreed. \"Just careful work.\"\n\nWe posted the menu that afternoon.\n\nThe first customer read it, frowned, and asked why the famous mountain mint tea had become so expensive.\n\nIroh explained the harvest without turning the farmers into a tragedy for entertainment.\n\n\"Then what is that one?\" the customer asked, pointing to the house blend.\n\n\"A new recipe,\" Iroh said. \"It may disappoint you.\"\n\nThe customer sniffed the cup.\n\n\"Smells good.\"\n\n\"It is intended to.\"\n\nThey tasted it.\n\n\"Hm.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"I'll have another.\"\n\nIroh's eyebrows rose.\n\nWe both tried not to look relieved.\n\nThe second customer complained that a famous shop shouldn't serve a cheaper alternative. The third asked if the house blend came in a travel flask. By evening, we'd sold more of it than we'd expected.\n\nNot everyone was pleased. That was normal. A business could make a fair decision and still disappoint someone.\n\nThe dispute wasn't over the price of mint. It was over a single line on the supplier's receipt: *Carriage cost to be absorbed by buyer.*\n\n“What does that mean?” Mara asked.\n\n“It means the merchant wants us to pay the additional cart fee,” Iroh said.\n\n“And if we don't?”\n\n“Then the growers may have to.”\n\nMina took the invoice from him. “Or the merchant is charging us the old fee plus a second one and hoping no one asks.”\n\nFor a moment Iroh looked offended on behalf of the whole system of trade. Then he smiled without much pleasure.\n\n“That,” he said, “is why I prefer a good ledger to a convincing speech.”\n\nWe sent a message asking the growers to confirm what they had agreed to and to whom they had already paid. The answer came back by evening: the carrier had raised his fee once, not twice. The extra line was an error made by a new clerk in the warehouse. We could settle the genuine cost without taking it out of anyone's harvest.\n\n“Easy fix,” I said.\n\n“It is easy only because Mina questioned it,” Iroh replied.\n\nShe was checking the sealed tins for damp leaves. “And because the farmers answered. Don't leave them out.”\n\nHe inclined his head. “Nor shall I.”\n\nThe next menu draft listed the actual shortage and price. It did not tell a story about a heroic shopkeeper saving a village. The growers had done their work. We were trying to do ours.\n\nMina brought the day's receipts over and helped us compare the cost of the new house blend with the old mountain mint. She'd been fielding customer complaints since morning and had a better idea than either of us about which substitutions people actually liked.\n\n\"We should write the reason for the change on the menu,\" she said. \"Not a sad story about the mountain. Just the supply shortage and the new price.\"\n\nIroh nodded. \"And the growers should be paid what they asked.\"\n\n\"Yes. That should be in the ledger, not in the speech you plan to make.\"\n\nHe looked wounded. I laughed into my sleeve.\n\nWe calculated what it would cost to keep one small tin of the rarer mint for customers who specifically wanted it. The rest of the menu could use local herbs whose sources had been checked. No one suggested sneaking unverified plants into a blend to make it seem luxurious.\n\nIroh wrote the numbers neatly, then looked up at me.\n\n\"Do you mind that our next evening won't be quite so extravagant?\"\n\n\"What did you plan?\"\n\n\"Tea. A walk. Possibly two sweet buns.\"\n\n\"That's a terrible disappointment.\"\n\nHis smile brightened. \"Two very good sweet buns.\"\n\nI reached across the counter to touch his hand. We had spoken openly about how I preferred polyamory and practiced ethical non-monogamy. A chosen evening together didn't become less intimate because I could love other people, or because the expensive mint had become scarce.\n\n\"Then I would like your company more than the rare tea,\" I said.\n\n\"An excellent investment.\"\n\nHe leaned close and waited. I kissed him briefly, then we returned to the till before Mina could accuse us of holding up the accounts. There were growers to pay and tomorrow's menu to prepare. This particular romance came with receipts.\n\nAfter closing, I helped Iroh count the till. He checked each coin twice, separated payment owed to workers from money needed for stock, and put a small sum in the repair reserve.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" I said.\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"I was remembering an old habit.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\n\"Thinking generosity meant giving something beautiful without asking what it cost.\"\n\nI leaned against the counter.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think generosity sometimes means saying the price aloud and making sure the people who grow the tea can eat.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That sounds like a lesson worth keeping.\"\n\nHe closed the ledger.\n\n\"Would you like to taste the final blend? I changed the citrus balance.\"\n\n\"Is that an invitation or a request for a quality-control volunteer?\"\n\n\"Both. I am very efficient.\"\n\nWe brewed the last test cup together.\n\nIt tasted of toasted grain, orange peel, and a touch of wild rosemary. It was not the expensive tea. It was good on its own terms.\n\n\"I like it,\" I said.\n\n\"You needn't be kind.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThen he pulled a small paper packet from his sleeve.\n\n\"One thing the supplier included.\"\n\nInside was a handful of dried mountain mint, no more than enough for a private pot.\n\n\"They sent a gift?\"\n\n\"A thank-you for accepting the reduced order.\"\n\nI touched the edge of the packet.\n\n\"Are you going to save it for someone important?\"\n\n\"I had thought of sharing it with someone important.\"\n\nI met his eyes.\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed it, then looked suddenly shy in a way that still surprised me after all our time together.\n\n\"Tomorrow evening?\" he asked.\n\n\"I would love that.\"\n\nHe tucked the packet away again. We walked home past the lanterns, with the new menu posted and the accounts balanced as far as they could be balanced.\n\nThe next day, a farmer from the supplier's district sent a brief note thanking the teahouse for paying the agreed price. It wasn't a blessing from the spirits or a sudden end to the shortage.\n\nIt was proof that someone on the other side of the road had been treated fairly.\n\nIroh placed the note beside the ledger.\n\nWhen the kettle boiled that evening, he opened the small packet.\n\nWe shared the mint without pretending it had cost nothing.","word_count":1843}};var series=lib.series.find(s=>s.series_slug==="jasmine-after-rain");if(!series)return;for(var i=0;i<series.chapters.length;i++){var c=series.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}series.total_word_count=80787;})();
